That sounds phun, but consider how complicated the alphabet would have
to grow. English has about 40 phonemes, many of which come in several
variants--that's a lot of letters you're looking for!

I doubt this complication would make things simpler or more elegant
really. In fact, I think English wins because of this loose mapping of
symbols to sounds. It allows for more variation, and that gives a richer
language. (If one started with 100 letters and a 1-to-1 mapping [and
isn't as stubborn as the Chinese] the alphabet would HAVE to evolve into
something simpler. If the 1-to-1 rule is carried on, this will cripple
the language. Actually, this is the case with many languages, including
English I guess, but to a lesser extent, because of this loose mapping.)

> Maybe the Chinese or Japanese are doing it right all along. ;)

Well, they do exactly the opposite: their systems are very far from a
1-to-1 mapping of symbols to sounds. When they DO want to transcribe
sounds, they use some of their symbols to represent syllables with fixed
pronunciation. This is a phonetically very poor system and leads for
example to Japanese pronunciations of English words with embedded extra
vowels and such, making them hardly recognizable. (The fun starts when
some of those transcribed terms find their way back into English.)

